@* Where the browser's own print furniture is turned off.

   The web address, the date and the page number across the top of a printed page are the
   browser's furniture, not part of the sheet — and no page can turn them off, because the
   setting belongs to whoever is printing. Saying where the switch is takes one line and is the
   difference between a sheet you would hand somebody and one you would not.

   **One component because two pages print a sheet**, and the sentence was identical in both. *@

<p class="small muted @Class">
    One page. Turn <b>Headers and footers</b> off under <b>More settings</b> to drop the
    browser's own address-and-date band.
</p>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Extra classes for the paragraph. The two callers differ here and should: the review step
    /// wants the spacing, and the sheet page wants the line gone when the sheet is printed.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }
}
